1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,630
Every month, small charges leave your
account.

2
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:07,913
A free trial that never ended.

3
00:00:08,176 --> 00:00:10,072
A plan that crept up in price.

4
00:00:10,213 --> 00:00:12,643
The same service billed twice, once on your
card and once through PayPal.

5
00:00:13,023 --> 00:00:16,954
On a statement full of coffee and groceries,
none of it stands out.

6
00:00:17,336 --> 00:00:18,872
SubSweep finds it.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,166
I drop in a statement CSV.

8
00:00:24,422 --> 00:00:27,199
It can come from a bank, a card or PayPal.

9
00:00:29,802 --> 00:00:35,024
SubSweep detects the format and cleans up
the messy merchant names, and it all stays

10
00:00:35,024 --> 00:00:36,081
on this computer.

11
00:00:36,316 --> 00:00:39,579
The only thing ever saved is which findings
I dismiss.

12
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,555
Here is the picture: ten subscriptions,
about seventeen hundred dollars a year, and

13
00:00:45,555 --> 00:00:47,322
a health score of forty-five.

14
00:00:47,611 --> 00:00:51,063
This is not machine learning, it is rules
you can read.

15
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:55,444
Groceries and fuel are excluded, and it says
which rule did that.

16
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:03,125
Open Netflix: every charge on a chart, the
step up where the price rose, and the reason

17
00:01:03,125 --> 00:01:04,548
SubSweep is confident.

18
00:01:05,502 --> 00:01:06,750
Now the findings.

19
00:01:07,004 --> 00:01:12,001
Streamly is billed twice: ten dollars a
month and a hundred a year, both through

20
00:01:12,001 --> 00:01:12,438
PayPal.

21
00:01:12,689 --> 00:01:16,699
The explanation is in plain English, with
the exact data behind it.

22
00:01:16,955 --> 00:01:22,044
Here the wording is built in; with an
Anthropic key, Claude writes it, and every

23
00:01:22,044 --> 00:01:23,570
figure is still checked.

24
00:01:23,823 --> 00:01:26,835
Keep one, and that saves about a hundred
dollars a year.

25
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,270
PayPal adds ground truth.

26
00:01:32,514 --> 00:01:38,040
FitPass Gym shows as cancelled in PayPal on
the eleventh of August, yet my statement has

27
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:40,426
two more charges, forty-eight dollars.

28
00:01:40,668 --> 00:01:43,136
A bank statement alone could never show
that.

29
00:01:43,602 --> 00:01:48,469
This panel is demo data; with sandbox
credentials it reads live from PayPal.

30
00:01:49,004 --> 00:01:52,831
SubSweep only reads status; it cannot cancel
anything.

31
00:01:57,504 --> 00:01:58,985
Let me act on the duplicate.

32
00:01:59,342 --> 00:02:05,207
SubSweep drafts a short, polite message,
with placeholders for my own account

33
00:02:05,207 --> 00:02:05,816
details.

34
00:02:06,061 --> 00:02:07,156
It is never sent.

35
00:02:07,252 --> 00:02:10,087
There is a copy button, and that is all.

36
00:02:10,427 --> 00:02:12,945
I decide what happens next.

37
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,645
And I can ask my own data a question.

38
00:02:18,939 --> 00:02:20,397
What do I spend on streaming?

39
00:02:20,534 --> 00:02:25,066
Twenty-six dollars a month, Hulu and
Netflix, with the data used right there.

40
00:02:25,302 --> 00:02:28,697
SubSweep: find what is quietly draining you.

41
00:02:28,738 --> 00:02:28,788
You decide.
